  "title": "Trans-Saharan gold and salt trade",
  "summary": "Camel caravans carried salt, gold and enslaved people between North Africa and West Africa. Large caravans took off in the 8th century CE and reached a golden period from the 9th. One route ran from Sijilmasa through Taghaza and Walata to Timbuktu, and the trade peaked in the 14th to 16th centuries under the Mali and Songhai empires.",
  "when": {
    "start": {
      "year": 701
    },
    "end": {
      "year": 1600
    },
    "precision": "century",
    "uncertainty": {
      "note": "Sources differ on the start: World History Encyclopedia says large caravans really took off from the 8th century CE (golden period from the 9th); Encyclopedia.com says regular trans-Saharan trade began in the fourth century. This record follows the 8th-century start; end is the close of the 16th century, the last century of the peak."
    },
